{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CdURGhEgd88E44tymfCNf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 8 icons synced successfully. Now I can write the design_spec.md. Let me compose the full Design Spec.\n\n**Deck plan - 12 slides**:\n1. P01 — Cover: \"Northwind Logistics Q3 FY26 Business Review\"\n2. P02 — Executive Summary: Key findings overview\n3. P03 — Revenue segment table: \"Freight and Last-Mile Drive 12.4% Revenue Growth to $47.2M\"\n4. P04 — Revenue trend chart: \"Revenue Has Grown Steadily to $47.2M Over Eight Quarters\"\n5. P05 — Gross margin bridge: \"Gross Margin Fell 180 bps to 29.4% on Fuel and Contract Labor\"\n6. P06 — Operating margin: \"Operating Margin Held at 8.1% as SG&A Growth Was Contained to 3.2%\"\n7. P07 — Customer concentration: \"Customer Concentration Improved for the Fourth Consecutive Quarter\"\n8. P08 — Last-mile: \"Last-Mile Is the Fastest-Growing Segment but Contribution Margin Remains Negative at −3.4%\"\n9. P09 — Headcount: \"Headcount Grew by 49 to 1,122\"\n10. P10 — Risks: \"Three Unhedged Risks Require Immediate Action\"\n11. P11 — Asks: \"Three Approvals Requested: Capex, Fuel Hedging, Engineering Headcount\"\n12. P12 — Q4 Outlook: \"Q4 Guidance of $48.5M–$50.0M with 60–90 bps Margin Recovery Expected\"\n\nNow I'm setting up the design specification with a professional color palette suited for financial reporting — using navy and steel-blue tones with light backgrounds for readability.\n\nDefining typography hierarchy... Now I'm mapping out the page structure—the cover and asks are anchor pages with breathing room, while most of the middle sections like revenue segments, margin analysis, and headcount are data-dense. Let me refine this breakdown to balance information density with visual pacing throughout the deck.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":760,"cache_read_input_tokens":90228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":760},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62f913d3-8d79-4b80-89f0-e9ab856e709b","uuid":"e89d98da-8af7-473b-8946-629a2fcf3cac","request_id":"req_011CdURGdNXAyjNzgStNcTsA"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CdURGhEgd88E44tymfCNf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 8 icons synced. Now writing the complete design_spec.md."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":760,"cache_read_input_tokens":90228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":760},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62f913d3-8d79-4b80-89f0-e9ab856e709b","uuid":"28cc61fd-8bd8-420a-bc12-ed1abac0935f","request_id":"req_011CdURGdNXAyjNzgStNcTsA"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CdURGhEgd88E44tymfCNf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012kjD1KC6aRpiFL8RvSMGFt","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/brycewang/Documents/GitHub/many-ppt-skills/benchmark/results/run-01/ppt-master/02-quarterly-review/rep-01/workspace/projects/northwind-q3-fy26-qbr_ppt169_20260728/design_spec.md","content":"<!-- ppt-master-schema: design-spec/v1 -->\n# Northwind Logistics Q3 FY26 QBR - Design Spec\n\n## I. Project Information\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project Name | northwind-q3-fy26-qbr_ppt169_20260728 |\n| Canvas Format | PPT 16:9 (1280 × 720) |\n| Page Count | 12 |\n| Target Audience | Northwind Logistics executive committee; C-suite decision-makers who are highly numerate, time-pressed, and accustomed to exact figures and confident assertions |\n| Communication Intent | Report Q3 FY26 financial performance with full variance accountability; expose three active risks; and obtain committee approval for three capital and operational decisions today |\n| Desired Audience Outcome | Committee understands the Q3 story (top-line beat plus margin compression drivers), owns the risk picture, and approves all three asks by end of the meeting |\n| Core Message / Ask / Action | Revenue grew 12.4% YoY to $47.2M but gross margin compressed 180 bps; three approvals—Reno capex, fuel hedging, and 8 engineering roles—are required to protect margin and accelerate growth |\n| Delivery Context | Primary: reader-led — circulated on laptops in the meeting room and must stand alone without a presenter. Secondary: brief live framing by management at session open |\n| Artifact Afterlife | Formal Q3 record; archived for board reporting and future-quarter variance baseline |\n| Reading Mode | text |\n| Content Strategy | Stay close — all source numbers must appear exactly as given; structure is tightened to a pyramid argument for executive clarity without introducing any figure not in the source material |\n| Design Style | Pyramid mode + data-journalism visual style; navy/steel-blue professional palette; dense multi-column layout with ruled separators and anchored KPI callouts |\n| Formula Policy | text-only |\n| AI Image Acquisition Path | not applicable |\n| Generation Mode | continuous |\n| Spec Refinement | disabled |\n| Created Date | 2026-07-28 |\n\n## II. Canvas Specification\n\n| Property | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Format | PPT 16:9 |\n| Dimensions | 1280 × 720 |\n| viewBox | `0 0 1280 720` |\n| Margins | 40px on all sides |\n| Content Area | x: 40–1240, y: 40–680; effective 1200 × 640 |\n\n## III. Visual Theme\n\n### Theme Style\n\n- **Mode**: pyramid\n- **Visual style**: data-journalism\n- **Theme**: High-density financial publication aesthetic — ruled hairlines, column grids, compact sidebar callouts, and source-line footnotes anchored to exact data values\n- **Tone**: Authoritative, precise, evidence-first; every assertion is immediately supported by a figure\n\n### Color Scheme\n\n| Role | HEX | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Background | #F7F8FA | Page field; near-white to maximize print and screen readability |\n| Secondary background | #EAECF0 | Alternating table rows, panel fills, sidebar backgrounds |\n| Primary | #1A2B4A | Dark navy; page title text, section headings, ruling lines |\n| Accent | #2D6A9F | Steel blue; key KPI callouts, chart bars/lines, hyperlinks |\n| Secondary accent | #E8A23E | Amber; risk highlights, negative-trend markers, warning labels |\n| Body text | #1F2937 | Near-black; body copy, table data cells, footnotes |\n\nAdditional recurring neutrals:\n\n| Role | HEX | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Surface | #FFFFFF | Panel lift for inset data cards and chart backgrounds |\n| Grid | #D1D5DB | Hairline table rules, column dividers |\n\n## IV. Typography System\n\n### Font Plan\n\n| Role | Chinese | English | Fallback tail |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Title | Microsoft YaHei | Calibri | Arial, sans-serif |\n| Body | Microsoft YaHei | Calibri | Arial, sans-serif |\n| Data | Microsoft YaHei | Consolas | Courier New, monospace |\n\n- **Title stack**: Calibri, Microsoft YaHei, Arial, sans-serif\n- **Body stack**: Calibri, Microsoft YaHei, Arial, sans-serif\n- **Data stack**: Consolas, Courier New, Microsoft YaHei, monospace\n\nRole rationale: A `data` role is added for table numeric cells — monospace ensures column alignment across revenue, headcount, and margin figures; it recurs on every data-table page.\n\n### Font Size Hierarchy\n\n| Purpose | Anchor Size (px) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Body | 20 |\n| Title | 34 |\n| Subtitle | 26 |\n| Lead | 24 |\n| Annotation | 16 |\n| Footnote | 13 |\n| Hero KPI | 52 |\n\n## V. Layout Principles\n\n### Page Structure\n\n- **Header area**: Page title in primary color at y ≈ 40–88; rule line (1px, #D1D5DB) immediately below separating header from content\n- **Content area**: y ≈ 96–672; full 1200px wide, subdivided by column grids as needed; left sidebar KPI callouts where content warrants\n- **Footer area**: Page number right-aligned at y ≈ 694; company name or section label left-aligned; rule at y ≈ 688\n\n### Spacing Specification\n\n| Element | Current Project |\n| --- | --- |\n| Safe margin | 40px |\n| Content block gap | 20px |\n| Icon-text gap | 10px |\n\n## VI. Icon Usage Specification\n\n- **Primary bundled library**: tabler-outline\n- **Stroke width**: 2\n\n| Purpose | Icon Path | Page |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Risk warning | tabler-outline/alert-triangle | P10 |\n| Approval/ask marker | tabler-outline/circle-check | P11 |\n| Revenue growth indicator | tabler-outline/trending-up | P02, P03 |\n| Margin decline indicator | tabler-outline/trending-down | P05 |\n| Headcount / people | tabler-outline/users-group | P09 |\n| Freight brokerage segment | tabler-outline/truck | P03 |\n| Warehousing segment | tabler-outline/building-warehouse | P03 |\n| Last-mile delivery segment | tabler-outline/truck-delivery | P08 |\n\n## VII. Visualization Reference List\n\n| Page | Template | Usage |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P03 | consulting_table | Segment revenue table with embedded YoY directional micro-visuals |\n| P04 | line_chart | Eight-quarter revenue trend on a continuous time axis |\n| P05 | waterfall_chart | Gross margin bridge from 31.2% to 29.4% via two driver bars |\n| P09 | basic_table | Headcount by function with QoQ change column |\n\n## VIII. Image Resource List\n\n| Filename | Dimensions | Ratio | Purpose | Type | Layout pattern | Crop Policy | Acquire Via | Status | Reference | text_policy | page_role |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## IX. Content Outline\n\n### Part 1: Opening\n\n#### Slide 01 - Cover\n\n- **Audience move**: Audience arrives at a meeting → immediately grasps the Q3 headline and the purpose of the session\n- **Layout**: Full-field dark-navy background (#1A2B4A) with centered typographic composition; large hero KPI number ($47.2M) anchored mid-page above the deck title; thin amber rule separating KPI from subtitle; company name and date in annotation weight at bottom\n- **Title**: Northwind Logistics — Q3 FY26 Business Review\n- **Core message**: Revenue reached $47.2M (+12.4% YoY); three decisions are required today\n- **Cover impact**: Hero number hook — \"$47.2M\" rendered at 80px in white on the dark navy field; positioned above the subtitle \"Q3 FY26 Business Review\"; amber underrule creates tension between the growth figure and the margin story below it; composition is typographic poster with one deliberate asymmetric rule\n- **Content**:\n  - Hero KPI: **$47.2M** (Q3 FY26 Revenue)\n  - Subtitle: Q3 FY26 Business Review\n  - Descriptor line: \"Revenue +12.4% YoY · Gross Margin −180 bps · Three Approvals Requested\"\n  - Company: Northwind Logistics\n  - Date: Q3 FY26 | July 2026\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 02 - Executive Summary\n\n- **Audience move**: Audience orients → holds the four key findings in working memory before the detail pages\n- **Layout**: Two-column grid; left column (≈ 580px) carries four KPI callout cards stacked vertically; right column (≈ 580px) carries the three session asks as a numbered approval list with amber numbered circles\n- **Title**: Four Findings, Three Approvals: The Q3 Story in Two Minutes\n- **Core message**: Revenue beat, margin missed, concentration improved, last-mile growing — and three decisions await\n- **Content**:\n\n  **Key Findings (left column)**\n  1. **Revenue: $47.2M, +12.4% YoY** — Q3 beat driven by freight brokerage (+15.9%) and last-mile (+23.4%)\n  2. **Gross margin: 29.4% (−180 bps)** — Fuel +14%/mi and contract labor 22% above plan compressed margin\n  3. **Customer concentration: Top-10 share fell to 38%** — Fourth consecutive quarter of improvement; 31 net new logos\n  4. **Operating margin: Held at 8.1%** — SG&A grew only 3.2% against 12.4% revenue growth\n\n  **Session Asks (right column)**\n  1. Approve $2.4M capex — Reno automation (15-month payback)\n  2. Approve fuel hedging — through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume\n  3. Approve 8 engineering roles — to accelerate last-mile routing rebuild\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Revenue Performance\n\n#### Slide 03 - Segment Revenue Table\n\n- **Audience move**: Overview → segment-level understanding of which lines drove growth and which contracted\n- **Layout**: Full-width ruling-line table occupying the content area; above the table a thin sub-header rule with segment icon row (four segment icons left-aligned); below the table a one-line callout strip showing total row highlighted in navy\n- **Title**: Freight and Last-Mile Drive $47.2M Q3 Revenue; Customs Declined Structurally\n- **Core message**: Three of four segments grew; customs fell 15.8% as two clients insourced — a structural, not cyclical, decline\n- **Visualization**: Consulting-table layout; embedded directional micro-bars in YoY column (positive = steel-blue, negative = amber)\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Table: Revenue by Segment\n\n  | Segment | Q3 FY26 | Q3 FY25 | YoY |\n  |---|---:|---:|---:|\n  | Freight brokerage | $24.1M | $20.8M | +15.9% |\n  | Warehousing | $13.6M | $12.9M | +5.4% |\n  | Last-mile delivery | $7.9M | $6.4M | +23.4% |\n  | Customs & compliance | $1.6M | $1.9M | −15.8% |\n  | **Total** | **$47.2M** | **$42.0M** | **+12.4%** |\n\n  Callout annotation (below table): \"Customs decline is structural — two clients insourced. Last-mile now represents 16.7% of revenue, up from 15.2%.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 04 - Revenue Trend\n\n- **Audience move**: Segment snapshot → longitudinal trend: audience sees a consistent upward trajectory confirming structural growth\n- **Layout**: Line chart occupying ≈ 65% of width on the left; right sidebar (≈ 35%) carries a mini data table of all 8 quarters plus a callout box for Q3 FY26 point highlight\n- **Title**: Revenue Has Grown Steadily to $47.2M Across Eight Quarters, with One Seasonal Dip in Q4 FY25\n- **Core message**: Q3 FY26 is the highest revenue quarter in the eight-quarter window at $47.2M; growth is structural\n- **Visualization**: Line chart — single series, 8 data points, annotated at Q3 FY26 ($47.2M), rule-line at Q4 FY25 dip ($41.2M)\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Chart data (eight quarters, $M):\n\n  | Quarter | Revenue ($M) |\n  |---|---:|\n  | Q4 FY24 | 36.1 |\n  | Q1 FY25 | 37.8 |\n  | Q2 FY25 | 39.9 |\n  | Q3 FY25 | 42.0 |\n  | Q4 FY25 | 41.2 |\n  | Q1 FY26 | 43.5 |\n  | Q2 FY26 | 45.1 |\n  | Q3 FY26 | 47.2 |\n\n  Annotation on chart: Final point labeled \"$47.2M (Q3 FY26)\"; dip point labeled \"Q4 FY25: $41.2M (seasonal)\".\n\n  Right sidebar mini-table: same 8 rows of data in compact format for reference.\n\n---\n\n### Part 3: Margin Analysis\n\n#### Slide 05 - Gross Margin Compression\n\n- **Audience move**: Revenue growth acknowledged → audience understands why profitability did not keep pace\n- **Layout**: Waterfall chart occupying ≈ 65% of width center-left; right sidebar shows the two-driver breakdown with large BPS callouts and icons\n- **Title**: Gross Margin Fell 180 bps to 29.4% on Fuel and Contract Labor; Both Drivers Are Addressable\n- **Core message**: Fuel alone cost −110 bps; peak-season contract labor cost −70 bps; combined 180 bps decline from 31.2% to 29.4%\n- **Visualization**: Waterfall chart — three bars: Start (31.2%), Fuel impact (−110 bps → intermediate), Labor impact (−70 bps → end 29.4%); start and end bars in navy; driver bars in amber\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Waterfall data:\n  - Start: Gross margin Q3 FY25 = **31.2%**\n  - Driver 1: Fuel — up 14% per mile against a flat contracted rate card → **−110 bps**\n  - Driver 2: Contract labor — peak-season warehouse staffing 22% above plan → **−70 bps**\n  - End: Gross margin Q3 FY26 = **29.4%**\n  - Total delta: **−180 bps**\n\n  Right sidebar callout boxes:\n  - Box 1: \"Fuel impact: −110 bps\" — rate card locked; hedging ask addresses forward exposure\n  - Box 2: \"Labor impact: −70 bps\" — peak-season roll-off expected in Q4; 60–90 bps recovery guided\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 06 - Operating Margin Defended\n\n- **Audience move**: Margin concern → reassurance that the company maintained operating discipline despite top-line cost pressure\n- **Layout**: Centered two-statistic layout; left KPI card (gross margin 29.4%); center explanation text block; right KPI card (operating margin 8.1%); below: SG&A efficiency callout strip across full width\n- **Title**: Operating Margin Held at 8.1% as SG&A Grew Only 3.2% Against 12.4% Revenue\n- **Core message**: Management absorbed gross margin pressure through SG&A discipline; operating leverage is intact\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left KPI: **Gross Margin: 29.4%** (−180 bps YoY) [amber labeled \"under pressure\"]\n  Center arrow/connector: \"→ offset by →\"\n  Right KPI: **Operating Margin: 8.1%** (held) [steel-blue labeled \"stable\"]\n\n  Explanation text block:\n  \"SG&A grew 3.2% in Q3 FY26 against 12.4% revenue growth, demonstrating operating leverage. Management absorbed the gross margin compression without allowing it to flow through to operating profit. This discipline provides runway to invest in last-mile and engineering while protecting the bottom line.\"\n\n  Efficiency callout (full-width strip):\n  Revenue growth: **+12.4%** | SG&A growth: **+3.2%** | Operating margin: **8.1%** (held)\n\n---\n\n### Part 4: Customer Base\n\n#### Slide 07 - Customer Concentration\n\n- **Audience move**: Margin story → portfolio quality: audience understands the business is de-risking from large-client dependency\n- **Layout**: Two-section layout; top section: two large metric cards side by side (top-10 share, new logos); bottom section: churn analysis two-column (count churn vs revenue churn) with interpretation text\n- **Title**: Customer Concentration Fell to 38% for the Fourth Consecutive Quarter; Net New Logos Grew 72%\n- **Core message**: Top-10 customer share fell from 44% to 38%; 31 net new logos vs 18 in Q3 FY25; churn was skewed to small accounts\n- **Content**:\n\n  Top metric cards:\n  - Card 1: **Top-10 Share: 38%** (was 44% Q3 FY25; −6pp YoY; 4th consecutive quarter of decline)\n  - Card 2: **31 Net New Logos** (vs 18 in Q3 FY25; +72% growth)\n\n  Churn analysis (bottom):\n  - Churn by count: **4.2%**\n  - Churn by revenue: **2.1%**\n  - Interpretation: \"Accounts lost were smaller than average — churn is occurring at the tail of the customer distribution, not the core. The revenue churn being half the count churn confirms healthy portfolio composition.\"\n\n---\n\n### Part 5: Last-Mile Deep Dive\n\n#### Slide 08 - Last-Mile Trajectory\n\n- **Audience move**: Portfolio overview → deep awareness that last-mile is the growth engine but still a cash consumer requiring investment\n- **Layout**: Left half: three stacked metric cards (revenue share, contribution margin, break-even); right half: explanatory text block with trajectory narrative and amber \"not yet profitable\" badge at top right\n- **Title**: Last-Mile Grew 23.4% and Now Represents 16.7% of Revenue but Contribution Margin Remains −3.4%\n- **Core message**: Last-mile is the fastest-growing segment; it is improving rapidly (from −7.9% to −3.4% contribution margin) and is projected to break even in Q2 FY27 at current trajectory\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left metric cards:\n  - Card 1: **Revenue share: 16.7%** (up from 15.2%; revenue $7.9M vs $6.4M; +23.4% YoY)\n  - Card 2: **Contribution margin: −3.4%** (improved from −7.9% a year ago; +450 bps improvement)\n  - Card 3: **Break-even: Q2 FY27** (projected at current trajectory)\n\n  Right text block:\n  \"Last-mile delivery is Northwind's highest-growth segment at +23.4% YoY, now representing 16.7% of total revenue, up from 15.2%. The segment is loss-making at −3.4% contribution margin but has improved by 450 bps year over year. At current trajectory, break-even is projected in Q2 FY27. The 8 incremental engineering roles requested today are intended to accelerate the last-mile routing rebuild, which is the primary lever for reaching contribution break-even ahead of schedule.\"\n\n  Badge (amber): \"NOT YET PROFITABLE — break-even Q2 FY27\"\n\n---\n\n### Part 6: People and Risk\n\n#### Slide 09 - Headcount\n\n- **Audience move**: Business performance → organizational capacity: audience confirms staffing has grown purposefully in the right functions\n- **Layout**: Full-width table with headcount data; above table: one-line KPI strip (total 1,122, +49 QoQ); below table: annotation on engineering growth and operations scale\n- **Title**: Headcount Grew by 49 to 1,122; Engineering and Operations Additions Are Targeted\n- **Core message**: Total headcount reached 1,122 (+49 QoQ); growth concentrated in Operations (+34) and Engineering (+11) to support last-mile expansion; G&A reduced by 2\n- **Visualization**: Table (basic_table) — 4 function rows plus total\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  KPI strip (above table): Total headcount: **1,122** | Net addition: **+49 QoQ**\n\n  Table: Headcount by Function\n\n  | Function | Q3 FY26 | Change QoQ |\n  |---|---:|---:|\n  | Operations | 812 | +34 |\n  | Engineering | 96 | +11 |\n  | Sales & marketing | 143 | +6 |\n  | G&A | 71 | −2 |\n  | **Total** | **1,122** | **+49** |\n\n  Annotation (below table): \"Engineering additions (+11 QoQ to 96 total) support the last-mile routing rebuild. The 8 additional roles requested today would bring Engineering to 104. G&A reduced by 2 — overhead discipline maintained.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 10 - Risk Register\n\n- **Audience move**: Performance data reviewed → audience confronts three specific risks that require management action and resource commitment\n- **Layout**: Three equal-width risk cards in a row; each card: amber alert-triangle icon at top, risk title, exposure detail, and mitigation lever; all three cards share equal prominence\n- **Title**: Three Unhedged Risks Require Immediate Management Action in Q4 FY26\n- **Core message**: Fuel exposure is open beyond Q1 FY27; two lease renewals cover 340k sq ft; customs decline is structural — all three require committee decisions or watchlist elevation\n- **Content**:\n\n  Risk card 1: **Fuel Exposure Unhedged Beyond Q1 FY27**\n  - Detail: A further 10% move in fuel costs an estimated $1.8M annualized\n  - Current position: Rate card is flat; exposure is fully unhedged beyond Q1 FY27\n  - Mitigation: Approve hedging through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume (ask #2)\n\n  Risk card 2: **Warehouse Lease Renewals in Memphis and Reno — Q4 FY26**\n  - Detail: 340,000 sq ft covered; leases last set at 2021 rates\n  - Timing: Both renewals land in Q4 FY26\n  - Mitigation: Approve Reno automation capex ($2.4M) to reduce footprint dependency (ask #1); Memphis renewal to be negotiated\n\n  Risk card 3: **Customs Segment Decline Is Structural**\n  - Detail: Revenue fell from $1.9M to $1.6M (−15.8%) as two clients insourced\n  - Nature: Structural, not cyclical — clients have built in-house capability\n  - Mitigation: No recovery expected; strategic options review recommended\n\n---\n\n### Part 7: Decisions and Outlook\n\n#### Slide 11 - Asks\n\n- **Audience move**: Risk awareness → action: audience reviews the three specific approvals requested and the rationale for each\n- **Layout**: Three stacked approval cards, each full width; circle-check icon left, ask title center, dollar amount or parameter right; rationale text beneath each in annotation size; approval status badge (\"PENDING APPROVAL\") right-aligned on each card\n- **Title**: Three Approvals Requested Today: Capex, Fuel Hedging, and Engineering Headcount\n- **Core message**: Committee approval is requested for $2.4M Reno automation capex, fuel hedging through Q4 FY27 at 60% of volume, and 8 engineering roles; all three have defined payback or protection rationale\n- **Content**:\n\n  Ask 1: **Approve $2.4M Reno Automation Capex**\n  - Rationale: Lease renewal creates the window to automate; 15-month payback projected; reduces variable labor dependency in a peak-capacity facility\n  - Amount: $2.4M capex\n\n  Ask 2: **Approve Fuel Hedging Through Q4 FY27 at Up to 60% of Projected Volume**\n  - Rationale: Current exposure is fully unhedged beyond Q1 FY27; a 10% price move costs $1.8M annualized; hedging at 60% caps the majority of the exposure\n  - Coverage: Through Q4 FY27 · Up to 60% of projected volume\n\n  Ask 3: **Approve 8 Incremental Engineering Roles**\n  - Rationale: Last-mile routing rebuild is the primary lever for contribution break-even in Q2 FY27; current Engineering headcount is 96; 8 roles brings team to 104 and accelerates the rebuild timeline\n  - Headcount: +8 engineers (Engineering total: 96 → 104)\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 12 - Q4 Outlook\n\n- **Audience move**: Decision session → forward view: audience leaves with a concrete Q4 expectation and confidence in margin recovery\n- **Layout**: Two-column layout; left (≈ 55%): guidance KPI cards stacked (revenue range, gross margin recovery); right (≈ 45%): explanatory text block plus one amber risk callout box at bottom right\n- **Title**: Q4 Guidance Is $48.5M–$50.0M with 60–90 bps Gross Margin Recovery as Peak Labor Rolls Off\n- **Core message**: Q4 revenue guidance of $48.5M–$50.0M; gross margin recovery of 60–90 bps expected as peak-season contract labor rolls off; fuel risk remains the main watch item\n- **Closing impact**: Forward call — the last thing the committee sees is a concrete, specific guidance range ($48.5M–$50.0M) paired with the margin recovery mechanism; this closes the session on a confident, evidence-anchored forward view rather than a generic \"thank you\" slide\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left KPI cards:\n  - Card 1: **Q4 Revenue Guidance: $48.5M–$50.0M**\n  - Card 2: **Gross Margin Recovery: +60–90 bps** (as peak-season contract labor rolls off)\n\n  Right text block:\n  \"Q4 FY26 revenue guidance is $48.5M–$50.0M, representing continued sequential growth. Gross margin is expected to recover 60–90 bps as peak-season contract labor normalizes post-Q3. This would bring gross margin back toward 30.0%–30.3%, partially reversing the Q3 compression. The primary watch item is fuel — the hedging approval requested today (ask #2) directly addresses forward fuel exposure beyond Q1 FY27.\"\n\n  Risk callout (amber box, bottom right):\n  \"⚠ Key watch item: Fuel exposure remains unhedged beyond Q1 FY27. Approval of hedging today (ask #2) closes this gap.\"\n\n## X. Speaker Notes Requirements\n\n- **Filename**: match each SVG filename under `notes/` (e.g., `01_cover.svg` → `notes/01_cover.md`)\n- **Content**: Each page's notes provide the presenter with: the audience move this page must achieve; the key numbers to call out verbatim; transition language to the next page; and any risk or objection the exec committee is likely to raise. Notes do not repeat slide content as prose — they provide context, transitions, and handling guidance.\n- **Total duration**: 20 minutes (reader-led; notes support a brief live framing only)\n- **Notes style**: formal\n- **Presentation purpose**: report and account; decide\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":760,"cache_read_input_tokens":90228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":760},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62f913d3-8d79-4b80-89f0-e9ab856e709b","uuid":"f5fa6bb0-0793-4c2a-a73c-5f6609c46701","request_id":"req_011CdURGdNXAyjNzgStNcTsA"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012kjD1KC6aRpiFL8RvSMGFt","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/brycewang/Documents/GitHub/many-ppt-skills/benchmark/results/run-01/ppt-master/02-quarterly-review/rep-01/workspace/projects/northwind-q3-fy26-qbr_ppt169_20260728/design_spec.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62f913d3-8d79-4b80-89f0-e9ab856e709b","uuid":"75893693-162e-41fc-8b71-e304c79ea9c9","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:03:33.871Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/brycewang/Documents/GitHub/many-ppt-skills/benchmark/results/run-01/ppt-master/02-quarterly-review/rep-01/workspace/projects/northwind-q3-fy26-qbr_ppt169_20260728/design_spec.md","content":"<!-- ppt-master-schema: design-spec/v1 -->\n# Northwind Logistics Q3 FY26 QBR - Design Spec\n\n## I. Project Information\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project Name | northwind-q3-fy26-qbr_ppt169_20260728 |\n| Canvas Format | PPT 16:9 (1280 × 720) |\n| Page Count | 12 |\n| Target Audience | Northwind Logistics executive committee; C-suite decision-makers who are highly numerate, time-pressed, and accustomed to exact figures and confident assertions |\n| Communication Intent | Report Q3 FY26 financial performance with full variance accountability; expose three active risks; and obtain committee approval for three capital and operational decisions today |\n| Desired Audience Outcome | Committee understands the Q3 story (top-line beat plus margin compression drivers), owns the risk picture, and approves all three asks by end of the meeting |\n| Core Message / Ask / Action | Revenue grew 12.4% YoY to $47.2M but gross margin compressed 180 bps; three approvals—Reno capex, fuel hedging, and 8 engineering roles—are required to protect margin and accelerate growth |\n| Delivery Context | Primary: reader-led — circulated on laptops in the meeting room and must stand alone without a presenter. Secondary: brief live framing by management at session open |\n| Artifact Afterlife | Formal Q3 record; archived for board reporting and future-quarter variance baseline |\n| Reading Mode | text |\n| Content Strategy | Stay close — all source numbers must appear exactly as given; structure is tightened to a pyramid argument for executive clarity without introducing any figure not in the source material |\n| Design Style | Pyramid mode + data-journalism visual style; navy/steel-blue professional palette; dense multi-column layout with ruled separators and anchored KPI callouts |\n| Formula Policy | text-only |\n| AI Image Acquisition Path | not applicable |\n| Generation Mode | continuous |\n| Spec Refinement | disabled |\n| Created Date | 2026-07-28 |\n\n## II. Canvas Specification\n\n| Property | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Format | PPT 16:9 |\n| Dimensions | 1280 × 720 |\n| viewBox | `0 0 1280 720` |\n| Margins | 40px on all sides |\n| Content Area | x: 40–1240, y: 40–680; effective 1200 × 640 |\n\n## III. Visual Theme\n\n### Theme Style\n\n- **Mode**: pyramid\n- **Visual style**: data-journalism\n- **Theme**: High-density financial publication aesthetic — ruled hairlines, column grids, compact sidebar callouts, and source-line footnotes anchored to exact data values\n- **Tone**: Authoritative, precise, evidence-first; every assertion is immediately supported by a figure\n\n### Color Scheme\n\n| Role | HEX | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Background | #F7F8FA | Page field; near-white to maximize print and screen readability |\n| Secondary background | #EAECF0 | Alternating table rows, panel fills, sidebar backgrounds |\n| Primary | #1A2B4A | Dark navy; page title text, section headings, ruling lines |\n| Accent | #2D6A9F | Steel blue; key KPI callouts, chart bars/lines, hyperlinks |\n| Secondary accent | #E8A23E | Amber; risk highlights, negative-trend markers, warning labels |\n| Body text | #1F2937 | Near-black; body copy, table data cells, footnotes |\n\nAdditional recurring neutrals:\n\n| Role | HEX | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Surface | #FFFFFF | Panel lift for inset data cards and chart backgrounds |\n| Grid | #D1D5DB | Hairline table rules, column dividers |\n\n## IV. Typography System\n\n### Font Plan\n\n| Role | Chinese | English | Fallback tail |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Title | Microsoft YaHei | Calibri | Arial, sans-serif |\n| Body | Microsoft YaHei | Calibri | Arial, sans-serif |\n| Data | Microsoft YaHei | Consolas | Courier New, monospace |\n\n- **Title stack**: Calibri, Microsoft YaHei, Arial, sans-serif\n- **Body stack**: Calibri, Microsoft YaHei, Arial, sans-serif\n- **Data stack**: Consolas, Courier New, Microsoft YaHei, monospace\n\nRole rationale: A `data` role is added for table numeric cells — monospace ensures column alignment across revenue, headcount, and margin figures; it recurs on every data-table page.\n\n### Font Size Hierarchy\n\n| Purpose | Anchor Size (px) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Body | 20 |\n| Title | 34 |\n| Subtitle | 26 |\n| Lead | 24 |\n| Annotation | 16 |\n| Footnote | 13 |\n| Hero KPI | 52 |\n\n## V. Layout Principles\n\n### Page Structure\n\n- **Header area**: Page title in primary color at y ≈ 40–88; rule line (1px, #D1D5DB) immediately below separating header from content\n- **Content area**: y ≈ 96–672; full 1200px wide, subdivided by column grids as needed; left sidebar KPI callouts where content warrants\n- **Footer area**: Page number right-aligned at y ≈ 694; company name or section label left-aligned; rule at y ≈ 688\n\n### Spacing Specification\n\n| Element | Current Project |\n| --- | --- |\n| Safe margin | 40px |\n| Content block gap | 20px |\n| Icon-text gap | 10px |\n\n## VI. Icon Usage Specification\n\n- **Primary bundled library**: tabler-outline\n- **Stroke width**: 2\n\n| Purpose | Icon Path | Page |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Risk warning | tabler-outline/alert-triangle | P10 |\n| Approval/ask marker | tabler-outline/circle-check | P11 |\n| Revenue growth indicator | tabler-outline/trending-up | P02, P03 |\n| Margin decline indicator | tabler-outline/trending-down | P05 |\n| Headcount / people | tabler-outline/users-group | P09 |\n| Freight brokerage segment | tabler-outline/truck | P03 |\n| Warehousing segment | tabler-outline/building-warehouse | P03 |\n| Last-mile delivery segment | tabler-outline/truck-delivery | P08 |\n\n## VII. Visualization Reference List\n\n| Page | Template | Usage |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P03 | consulting_table | Segment revenue table with embedded YoY directional micro-visuals |\n| P04 | line_chart | Eight-quarter revenue trend on a continuous time axis |\n| P05 | waterfall_chart | Gross margin bridge from 31.2% to 29.4% via two driver bars |\n| P09 | basic_table | Headcount by function with QoQ change column |\n\n## VIII. Image Resource List\n\n| Filename | Dimensions | Ratio | Purpose | Type | Layout pattern | Crop Policy | Acquire Via | Status | Reference | text_policy | page_role |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## IX. Content Outline\n\n### Part 1: Opening\n\n#### Slide 01 - Cover\n\n- **Audience move**: Audience arrives at a meeting → immediately grasps the Q3 headline and the purpose of the session\n- **Layout**: Full-field dark-navy background (#1A2B4A) with centered typographic composition; large hero KPI number ($47.2M) anchored mid-page above the deck title; thin amber rule separating KPI from subtitle; company name and date in annotation weight at bottom\n- **Title**: Northwind Logistics — Q3 FY26 Business Review\n- **Core message**: Revenue reached $47.2M (+12.4% YoY); three decisions are required today\n- **Cover impact**: Hero number hook — \"$47.2M\" rendered at 80px in white on the dark navy field; positioned above the subtitle \"Q3 FY26 Business Review\"; amber underrule creates tension between the growth figure and the margin story below it; composition is typographic poster with one deliberate asymmetric rule\n- **Content**:\n  - Hero KPI: **$47.2M** (Q3 FY26 Revenue)\n  - Subtitle: Q3 FY26 Business Review\n  - Descriptor line: \"Revenue +12.4% YoY · Gross Margin −180 bps · Three Approvals Requested\"\n  - Company: Northwind Logistics\n  - Date: Q3 FY26 | July 2026\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 02 - Executive Summary\n\n- **Audience move**: Audience orients → holds the four key findings in working memory before the detail pages\n- **Layout**: Two-column grid; left column (≈ 580px) carries four KPI callout cards stacked vertically; right column (≈ 580px) carries the three session asks as a numbered approval list with amber numbered circles\n- **Title**: Four Findings, Three Approvals: The Q3 Story in Two Minutes\n- **Core message**: Revenue beat, margin missed, concentration improved, last-mile growing — and three decisions await\n- **Content**:\n\n  **Key Findings (left column)**\n  1. **Revenue: $47.2M, +12.4% YoY** — Q3 beat driven by freight brokerage (+15.9%) and last-mile (+23.4%)\n  2. **Gross margin: 29.4% (−180 bps)** — Fuel +14%/mi and contract labor 22% above plan compressed margin\n  3. **Customer concentration: Top-10 share fell to 38%** — Fourth consecutive quarter of improvement; 31 net new logos\n  4. **Operating margin: Held at 8.1%** — SG&A grew only 3.2% against 12.4% revenue growth\n\n  **Session Asks (right column)**\n  1. Approve $2.4M capex — Reno automation (15-month payback)\n  2. Approve fuel hedging — through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume\n  3. Approve 8 engineering roles — to accelerate last-mile routing rebuild\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Revenue Performance\n\n#### Slide 03 - Segment Revenue Table\n\n- **Audience move**: Overview → segment-level understanding of which lines drove growth and which contracted\n- **Layout**: Full-width ruling-line table occupying the content area; above the table a thin sub-header rule with segment icon row (four segment icons left-aligned); below the table a one-line callout strip showing total row highlighted in navy\n- **Title**: Freight and Last-Mile Drive $47.2M Q3 Revenue; Customs Declined Structurally\n- **Core message**: Three of four segments grew; customs fell 15.8% as two clients insourced — a structural, not cyclical, decline\n- **Visualization**: Consulting-table layout; embedded directional micro-bars in YoY column (positive = steel-blue, negative = amber)\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Table: Revenue by Segment\n\n  | Segment | Q3 FY26 | Q3 FY25 | YoY |\n  |---|---:|---:|---:|\n  | Freight brokerage | $24.1M | $20.8M | +15.9% |\n  | Warehousing | $13.6M | $12.9M | +5.4% |\n  | Last-mile delivery | $7.9M | $6.4M | +23.4% |\n  | Customs & compliance | $1.6M | $1.9M | −15.8% |\n  | **Total** | **$47.2M** | **$42.0M** | **+12.4%** |\n\n  Callout annotation (below table): \"Customs decline is structural — two clients insourced. Last-mile now represents 16.7% of revenue, up from 15.2%.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 04 - Revenue Trend\n\n- **Audience move**: Segment snapshot → longitudinal trend: audience sees a consistent upward trajectory confirming structural growth\n- **Layout**: Line chart occupying ≈ 65% of width on the left; right sidebar (≈ 35%) carries a mini data table of all 8 quarters plus a callout box for Q3 FY26 point highlight\n- **Title**: Revenue Has Grown Steadily to $47.2M Across Eight Quarters, with One Seasonal Dip in Q4 FY25\n- **Core message**: Q3 FY26 is the highest revenue quarter in the eight-quarter window at $47.2M; growth is structural\n- **Visualization**: Line chart — single series, 8 data points, annotated at Q3 FY26 ($47.2M), rule-line at Q4 FY25 dip ($41.2M)\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Chart data (eight quarters, $M):\n\n  | Quarter | Revenue ($M) |\n  |---|---:|\n  | Q4 FY24 | 36.1 |\n  | Q1 FY25 | 37.8 |\n  | Q2 FY25 | 39.9 |\n  | Q3 FY25 | 42.0 |\n  | Q4 FY25 | 41.2 |\n  | Q1 FY26 | 43.5 |\n  | Q2 FY26 | 45.1 |\n  | Q3 FY26 | 47.2 |\n\n  Annotation on chart: Final point labeled \"$47.2M (Q3 FY26)\"; dip point labeled \"Q4 FY25: $41.2M (seasonal)\".\n\n  Right sidebar mini-table: same 8 rows of data in compact format for reference.\n\n---\n\n### Part 3: Margin Analysis\n\n#### Slide 05 - Gross Margin Compression\n\n- **Audience move**: Revenue growth acknowledged → audience understands why profitability did not keep pace\n- **Layout**: Waterfall chart occupying ≈ 65% of width center-left; right sidebar shows the two-driver breakdown with large BPS callouts and icons\n- **Title**: Gross Margin Fell 180 bps to 29.4% on Fuel and Contract Labor; Both Drivers Are Addressable\n- **Core message**: Fuel alone cost −110 bps; peak-season contract labor cost −70 bps; combined 180 bps decline from 31.2% to 29.4%\n- **Visualization**: Waterfall chart — three bars: Start (31.2%), Fuel impact (−110 bps → intermediate), Labor impact (−70 bps → end 29.4%); start and end bars in navy; driver bars in amber\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  Waterfall data:\n  - Start: Gross margin Q3 FY25 = **31.2%**\n  - Driver 1: Fuel — up 14% per mile against a flat contracted rate card → **−110 bps**\n  - Driver 2: Contract labor — peak-season warehouse staffing 22% above plan → **−70 bps**\n  - End: Gross margin Q3 FY26 = **29.4%**\n  - Total delta: **−180 bps**\n\n  Right sidebar callout boxes:\n  - Box 1: \"Fuel impact: −110 bps\" — rate card locked; hedging ask addresses forward exposure\n  - Box 2: \"Labor impact: −70 bps\" — peak-season roll-off expected in Q4; 60–90 bps recovery guided\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 06 - Operating Margin Defended\n\n- **Audience move**: Margin concern → reassurance that the company maintained operating discipline despite top-line cost pressure\n- **Layout**: Centered two-statistic layout; left KPI card (gross margin 29.4%); center explanation text block; right KPI card (operating margin 8.1%); below: SG&A efficiency callout strip across full width\n- **Title**: Operating Margin Held at 8.1% as SG&A Grew Only 3.2% Against 12.4% Revenue\n- **Core message**: Management absorbed gross margin pressure through SG&A discipline; operating leverage is intact\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left KPI: **Gross Margin: 29.4%** (−180 bps YoY) [amber labeled \"under pressure\"]\n  Center arrow/connector: \"→ offset by →\"\n  Right KPI: **Operating Margin: 8.1%** (held) [steel-blue labeled \"stable\"]\n\n  Explanation text block:\n  \"SG&A grew 3.2% in Q3 FY26 against 12.4% revenue growth, demonstrating operating leverage. Management absorbed the gross margin compression without allowing it to flow through to operating profit. This discipline provides runway to invest in last-mile and engineering while protecting the bottom line.\"\n\n  Efficiency callout (full-width strip):\n  Revenue growth: **+12.4%** | SG&A growth: **+3.2%** | Operating margin: **8.1%** (held)\n\n---\n\n### Part 4: Customer Base\n\n#### Slide 07 - Customer Concentration\n\n- **Audience move**: Margin story → portfolio quality: audience understands the business is de-risking from large-client dependency\n- **Layout**: Two-section layout; top section: two large metric cards side by side (top-10 share, new logos); bottom section: churn analysis two-column (count churn vs revenue churn) with interpretation text\n- **Title**: Customer Concentration Fell to 38% for the Fourth Consecutive Quarter; Net New Logos Grew 72%\n- **Core message**: Top-10 customer share fell from 44% to 38%; 31 net new logos vs 18 in Q3 FY25; churn was skewed to small accounts\n- **Content**:\n\n  Top metric cards:\n  - Card 1: **Top-10 Share: 38%** (was 44% Q3 FY25; −6pp YoY; 4th consecutive quarter of decline)\n  - Card 2: **31 Net New Logos** (vs 18 in Q3 FY25; +72% growth)\n\n  Churn analysis (bottom):\n  - Churn by count: **4.2%**\n  - Churn by revenue: **2.1%**\n  - Interpretation: \"Accounts lost were smaller than average — churn is occurring at the tail of the customer distribution, not the core. The revenue churn being half the count churn confirms healthy portfolio composition.\"\n\n---\n\n### Part 5: Last-Mile Deep Dive\n\n#### Slide 08 - Last-Mile Trajectory\n\n- **Audience move**: Portfolio overview → deep awareness that last-mile is the growth engine but still a cash consumer requiring investment\n- **Layout**: Left half: three stacked metric cards (revenue share, contribution margin, break-even); right half: explanatory text block with trajectory narrative and amber \"not yet profitable\" badge at top right\n- **Title**: Last-Mile Grew 23.4% and Now Represents 16.7% of Revenue but Contribution Margin Remains −3.4%\n- **Core message**: Last-mile is the fastest-growing segment; it is improving rapidly (from −7.9% to −3.4% contribution margin) and is projected to break even in Q2 FY27 at current trajectory\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left metric cards:\n  - Card 1: **Revenue share: 16.7%** (up from 15.2%; revenue $7.9M vs $6.4M; +23.4% YoY)\n  - Card 2: **Contribution margin: −3.4%** (improved from −7.9% a year ago; +450 bps improvement)\n  - Card 3: **Break-even: Q2 FY27** (projected at current trajectory)\n\n  Right text block:\n  \"Last-mile delivery is Northwind's highest-growth segment at +23.4% YoY, now representing 16.7% of total revenue, up from 15.2%. The segment is loss-making at −3.4% contribution margin but has improved by 450 bps year over year. At current trajectory, break-even is projected in Q2 FY27. The 8 incremental engineering roles requested today are intended to accelerate the last-mile routing rebuild, which is the primary lever for reaching contribution break-even ahead of schedule.\"\n\n  Badge (amber): \"NOT YET PROFITABLE — break-even Q2 FY27\"\n\n---\n\n### Part 6: People and Risk\n\n#### Slide 09 - Headcount\n\n- **Audience move**: Business performance → organizational capacity: audience confirms staffing has grown purposefully in the right functions\n- **Layout**: Full-width table with headcount data; above table: one-line KPI strip (total 1,122, +49 QoQ); below table: annotation on engineering growth and operations scale\n- **Title**: Headcount Grew by 49 to 1,122; Engineering and Operations Additions Are Targeted\n- **Core message**: Total headcount reached 1,122 (+49 QoQ); growth concentrated in Operations (+34) and Engineering (+11) to support last-mile expansion; G&A reduced by 2\n- **Visualization**: Table (basic_table) — 4 function rows plus total\n- **Native-ready**: yes\n- **Content**:\n\n  KPI strip (above table): Total headcount: **1,122** | Net addition: **+49 QoQ**\n\n  Table: Headcount by Function\n\n  | Function | Q3 FY26 | Change QoQ |\n  |---|---:|---:|\n  | Operations | 812 | +34 |\n  | Engineering | 96 | +11 |\n  | Sales & marketing | 143 | +6 |\n  | G&A | 71 | −2 |\n  | **Total** | **1,122** | **+49** |\n\n  Annotation (below table): \"Engineering additions (+11 QoQ to 96 total) support the last-mile routing rebuild. The 8 additional roles requested today would bring Engineering to 104. G&A reduced by 2 — overhead discipline maintained.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 10 - Risk Register\n\n- **Audience move**: Performance data reviewed → audience confronts three specific risks that require management action and resource commitment\n- **Layout**: Three equal-width risk cards in a row; each card: amber alert-triangle icon at top, risk title, exposure detail, and mitigation lever; all three cards share equal prominence\n- **Title**: Three Unhedged Risks Require Immediate Management Action in Q4 FY26\n- **Core message**: Fuel exposure is open beyond Q1 FY27; two lease renewals cover 340k sq ft; customs decline is structural — all three require committee decisions or watchlist elevation\n- **Content**:\n\n  Risk card 1: **Fuel Exposure Unhedged Beyond Q1 FY27**\n  - Detail: A further 10% move in fuel costs an estimated $1.8M annualized\n  - Current position: Rate card is flat; exposure is fully unhedged beyond Q1 FY27\n  - Mitigation: Approve hedging through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume (ask #2)\n\n  Risk card 2: **Warehouse Lease Renewals in Memphis and Reno — Q4 FY26**\n  - Detail: 340,000 sq ft covered; leases last set at 2021 rates\n  - Timing: Both renewals land in Q4 FY26\n  - Mitigation: Approve Reno automation capex ($2.4M) to reduce footprint dependency (ask #1); Memphis renewal to be negotiated\n\n  Risk card 3: **Customs Segment Decline Is Structural**\n  - Detail: Revenue fell from $1.9M to $1.6M (−15.8%) as two clients insourced\n  - Nature: Structural, not cyclical — clients have built in-house capability\n  - Mitigation: No recovery expected; strategic options review recommended\n\n---\n\n### Part 7: Decisions and Outlook\n\n#### Slide 11 - Asks\n\n- **Audience move**: Risk awareness → action: audience reviews the three specific approvals requested and the rationale for each\n- **Layout**: Three stacked approval cards, each full width; circle-check icon left, ask title center, dollar amount or parameter right; rationale text beneath each in annotation size; approval status badge (\"PENDING APPROVAL\") right-aligned on each card\n- **Title**: Three Approvals Requested Today: Capex, Fuel Hedging, and Engineering Headcount\n- **Core message**: Committee approval is requested for $2.4M Reno automation capex, fuel hedging through Q4 FY27 at 60% of volume, and 8 engineering roles; all three have defined payback or protection rationale\n- **Content**:\n\n  Ask 1: **Approve $2.4M Reno Automation Capex**\n  - Rationale: Lease renewal creates the window to automate; 15-month payback projected; reduces variable labor dependency in a peak-capacity facility\n  - Amount: $2.4M capex\n\n  Ask 2: **Approve Fuel Hedging Through Q4 FY27 at Up to 60% of Projected Volume**\n  - Rationale: Current exposure is fully unhedged beyond Q1 FY27; a 10% price move costs $1.8M annualized; hedging at 60% caps the majority of the exposure\n  - Coverage: Through Q4 FY27 · Up to 60% of projected volume\n\n  Ask 3: **Approve 8 Incremental Engineering Roles**\n  - Rationale: Last-mile routing rebuild is the primary lever for contribution break-even in Q2 FY27; current Engineering headcount is 96; 8 roles brings team to 104 and accelerates the rebuild timeline\n  - Headcount: +8 engineers (Engineering total: 96 → 104)\n\n---\n\n#### Slide 12 - Q4 Outlook\n\n- **Audience move**: Decision session → forward view: audience leaves with a concrete Q4 expectation and confidence in margin recovery\n- **Layout**: Two-column layout; left (≈ 55%): guidance KPI cards stacked (revenue range, gross margin recovery); right (≈ 45%): explanatory text block plus one amber risk callout box at bottom right\n- **Title**: Q4 Guidance Is $48.5M–$50.0M with 60–90 bps Gross Margin Recovery as Peak Labor Rolls Off\n- **Core message**: Q4 revenue guidance of $48.5M–$50.0M; gross margin recovery of 60–90 bps expected as peak-season contract labor rolls off; fuel risk remains the main watch item\n- **Closing impact**: Forward call — the last thing the committee sees is a concrete, specific guidance range ($48.5M–$50.0M) paired with the margin recovery mechanism; this closes the session on a confident, evidence-anchored forward view rather than a generic \"thank you\" slide\n- **Content**:\n\n  Left KPI cards:\n  - Card 1: **Q4 Revenue Guidance: $48.5M–$50.0M**\n  - Card 2: **Gross Margin Recovery: +60–90 bps** (as peak-season contract labor rolls off)\n\n  Right text block:\n  \"Q4 FY26 revenue guidance is $48.5M–$50.0M, representing continued sequential growth. Gross margin is expected to recover 60–90 bps as peak-season contract labor normalizes post-Q3. This would bring gross margin back toward 30.0%–30.3%, partially reversing the Q3 compression. The primary watch item is fuel — the hedging approval requested today (ask #2) directly addresses forward fuel exposure beyond Q1 FY27.\"\n\n  Risk callout (amber box, bottom right):\n  \"⚠ Key watch item: Fuel exposure remains unhedged beyond Q1 FY27. Approval of hedging today (ask #2) closes this gap.\"\n\n## X. Speaker Notes Requirements\n\n- **Filename**: match each SVG filename under `notes/` (e.g., `01_cover.svg` → `notes/01_cover.md`)\n- **Content**: Each page's notes provide the presenter with: the audience move this page must achieve; the key numbers to call out verbatim; transition language to the next page; and any risk or objection the exec committee is likely to raise. Notes do not repeat slide content as prose — they provide context, transitions, and handling guidance.\n- **Total duration**: 20 minutes (reader-led; notes support a brief live framing only)\n- **Notes style**: formal\n- **Presentation purpose**: report and account; decide\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
